## Men's Sexual Health: Common Problems, Causes, Treatment and Prevention

Sexual health is not only about sexual performance. It also includes sexual desire, erections, ejaculation, fertility, hormone health, emotional well-being, and healthy relationships.

Many men experience sexual health concerns at some point. Erectile dysfunction, low libido, premature ejaculation, low testosterone, and fertility problems are among the concerns that can affect sexual function.

A man may also notice a change in sexual function because of diabetes, high blood pressure, certain medicines, stress, hormonal problems, or other health conditions. **These problems should not simply be dismissed as a normal part of getting older.**

For men who are worried about sexual weakness, changes in erections, ejaculation problems, or fertility, finding the underlying cause is often more useful than trying unproven products or supplements.

## What Does Men's Sexual Health Include?

Men's sexual health covers physical, hormonal, reproductive, psychological, and relationship-related aspects of sexual well-being. Common concerns include erectile dysfunction, low sexual desire, premature ejaculation, ejaculation problems, low testosterone, penile problems, sexually transmitted infections, and male infertility.

Sexual health is closely connected with general health. For example, erectile dysfunction can sometimes occur alongside conditions affecting blood vessels, hormones, nerves, or metabolism.

## What Are the Most Common Men's Sexual Health Problems?

Men's sexual health issues can involve desire, erections, ejaculation, orgasm, fertility, or pain. Some problems occur occasionally, while persistent or recurring symptoms may need medical assessment.

Common concerns include:

| Sexual health concern | What it may involve |
|---|---|
| Erectile dysfunction | Difficulty getting or keeping an erection firm enough for sex |
| Low libido | Reduced interest or desire for sexual activity |
| Premature ejaculation | Ejaculation that happens sooner than desired and causes distress |
| Ejaculation problems | Difficulty ejaculating, delayed ejaculation, or ejaculation occurring without normal semen release |
| Low testosterone | Reduced testosterone levels that may be associated with low desire and other symptoms |
| Penile curvature | A bend in the penis that may sometimes cause pain or difficulty with intercourse |
| Male infertility | Difficulty achieving pregnancy because of problems involving sperm production, delivery, or reproductive function |
| Sexual pain | Pain involving the penis, testicles, pelvis, or sexual activity |

Sexual dysfunction does not always have one single cause. Physical health, medicines, hormones, emotional factors, and lifestyle can all contribute.

## What Causes Sexual Health Problems in Men?

Men's sexual health problems can have physical, hormonal, psychological, medication-related, and lifestyle-related causes.

Some common factors include:

* Diabetes
* High blood pressure
* Cardiovascular or blood-vessel disease
* Obesity and excess weight
* Low testosterone or other hormone problems
* Thyroid disorders
* Nerve disorders or nerve damage
* Certain prescription medicines
* Smoking
* Excessive alcohol use
* Recreational drug use
* Stress and anxiety
* Depression
* Relationship difficulties
* Previous pelvic surgery or injury

Erectile dysfunction, for example, can result from problems involving blood vessels, nerves, hormones, medicines, mental health, or lifestyle factors.

This is why treating sexual weakness without understanding its cause may not address the actual problem.

## Can Erectile Dysfunction Be a Sign of Another Health Problem?

Yes. Erectile dysfunction can sometimes be an early sign of an underlying health condition, particularly when it develops repeatedly.

An erection depends on adequate blood flow, healthy nerves, appropriate hormone function, and sexual stimulation. Problems affecting these systems can interfere with erectile function.

Conditions associated with erectile dysfunction include diabetes, high blood pressure, cardiovascular disease, kidney disease, obesity, low testosterone, thyroid problems, and some neurological disorders. Certain medicines can also contribute.

Occasional difficulty getting an erection can happen because of stress, tiredness, or alcohol. Repeated problems deserve more attention, particularly when they are new or becoming worse.

